<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pan Pan</h1><p class="st-meta">424 CE – 700 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Indianised kingdom on the Malay Peninsula that sent envoys to China in the 5th to 7th centuries and was known for its Brahmins and Buddhist monks.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pan Pan appears in Chinese dynastic histories from 424, when it began sending tribute missions to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liu-song">Liu Song</a> court. Its location is uncertain: most scholars place it on the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, perhaps around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bandon">Bay of Bandon</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat-thani">Surat Thani</a>, though <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kelantan-sultanate">Kelantan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terengganu-sultanate">Terengganu</a> have also been proposed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chinese sources describe a kingdom where many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> lived and were honoured by the king, and where there were Buddhist monasteries and monks who studied the scriptures. Like other peninsular ports, it profited from trade crossing the isthmus between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, offering aromatics and other local goods.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Missions from Pan Pan stop being recorded after the 7th century, and it was probably absorbed into larger networks such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> or the states that became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tambralinga">Tambralinga</a>. Its main interest today lies in what it shows of early Indian religious influence on the peninsula.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 424 CE – 700 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:68.14%;width:5.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>424 CE – 700 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Panpan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=562&amp;focus=pan-pan" aria-label="Pan Pan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7440 1595 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pan Pan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7760 1851h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7760 1851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Thailand. <a href="/atlas?year=562&amp;focus=pan-pan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

